{"name":"Farsighted","hex":"#e5e3ef","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"farsighted","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unable to focus with one's eyes on near objects; presbyopic.","Considering the future with respect to one's own plans or deeds; showing anticipation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/farsighted"}],"entries":[{"title":"Farsighted (book)","blocks":[{"text":"Farsighted: How We Make the Decisions That Matter the Most (2018) is a nonfiction book by American journalist Steven Johnson."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fe/FarsightedBook.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farsighted_(book)"},{"title":"Farsightedness","blocks":[{"text":"Far-sightedness, also known as long-sightedness, hypermetropia, and hyperopia, is a condition of the eye where distant objects are seen clearly but near objects appear blurred. This blur is due to incoming light being focused behind, instead of on, the retina due to insufficient accommodation by the lens. Minor hypermetropia in young patients is usually corrected by their accommodation, without any defects in vision. But, due to this accommodative effort for distant vision, people may complain of eye strain during prolonged reading. If the hypermetropia is high, there will be defective vision for both distance and near. People may also experience accommodative dysfunction, binocular dysfunction, amblyopia, and strabismus. Newborns are almost invariably hypermetropic, but it gradually decreases as the newborn gets older."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Hypermetropia_color.svg/330px-Hypermetropia_color.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farsightedness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Hyperopia_comparison.jpg/500px-Hyperopia_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Hyperopia_comparison.jpg/1280px-Hyperopia_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Hyperopia_comparison.jpg/500px-Hyperopia_comparison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Far-sighted vision on left, normal vision on right","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHyperopia_comparison.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Human_eye_cross-sectional_view_grayscale.png/330px-Human_eye_cross-sectional_view_grayscale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Human_eye_cross-sectional_view_grayscale.png/330px-Human_eye_cross-sectional_view_grayscale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human eye cross-section","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman_eye_cross-sectional_view_grayscale.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/1999_Choroid_folds.jpg/500px-1999_Choroid_folds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/1999_Choroid_folds.jpg/500px-1999_Choroid_folds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Choroid folds in high hyperopia (fluorescein angiography)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1999_Choroid_folds.jpg"}],"sounds":{"farsighted":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-farsighted.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/w